          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe about exercise and anxiety?",
          "answer": "The guidance I searched does not describe exercise as a treatment for anxiety. None of the retrieved passages address that question directly, so I can't tell you what it says.\n\nThe closest document, \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care,\" covers screening for anxiety in adults. It also describes the seven-item GAD-7 questionnaire, which is used to measure anxiety severity. Its commonly used bands are 5 for mild, 10 for moderate, and 15 for severe, with 10 usually the point where further assessment follows.\n\nOther documents mention exercise, but for other conditions. The osteoarthritis guidance recommends structured exercise for knee and hip pain, and the coronary artery disease guidance recommends supervised exercise after a heart diagnosis. Neither says anything about anxiety.\n\nIf you want to know whether exercise might help anxiety, a doctor or other clinician is the right person to ask. They can consider your situation as a whole.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
